# Line endings are a portability contract, not a preference.
#
# Git for Windows installs with `core.autocrlf=true`, so without this file a
# Windows clone rewrites every text file to CRLF on checkout. Everything in
# this repository is read by a Linux container, and a stray \r breaks them in
# ways that do not name themselves:
#
#   backend/entrypoint.sh  -> `\r` on the shebang line, so Docker reports
#                             "exec /entrypoint.sh: no such file or directory"
#                             and the backend restarts forever
#   .env / .env.example    -> values arrive with a trailing \r, so
#                             DJANGO_ALLOWED_HOSTS never matches and NGINX_PORT
#                             is not a number
#   nginx/*.conf           -> directives parse, but only by nginx's goodwill
#
# `eol=lf` means: normalise to LF in the repository *and* check out LF on every
# platform. Windows editors have handled LF for a decade; containers have not
# handled CRLF ever.
#
# If you cloned before this file existed, your working tree still has CRLF.
# Fix it once, from the repository root:
#
#     git add --renormalize .
#     git checkout -- .
#
# The build no longer depends on your having done so — backend/Dockerfile
# strips CR from the entrypoint itself — but everything else in the tree does.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Spelled out for the files where a wrong ending is fatal rather than untidy.
*.sh          text eol=lf
Dockerfile    text eol=lf
*.conf        text eol=lf
.env.example  text eol=lf

# Never touched: Git guesses well from content, but not on an empty file, and a
# corrupted upload fixture is a bad afternoon.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.eot   binary
